00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:17.670
<v Ira Glass>The day Sandra should have been dead started like a normal day. She was cutting school with the girls in her gang.
<v Ira Glass>桑德拉本该死掉的那一天，是以平凡的一天开始的。她当时正和帮派里的女孩们一起逃学。

00:00:17.670 --> 00:01:09.400
<v Sandra>We were standing there, and a group of girls passed us, and they looked at us, and we looked at them. And of course, automatically our mouths flew open without thinking. What are you looking at? Oh, you got something? What do you be about? And started asking them what their affiliation is, and other stuff. And we started talking [BLEEP] to them and other stuff. And they pulled out a gun, and they said OK, you want to talk [BLEEP] now? Let's talk [BLEEP]. And I panicked. I mean, nobody had ever pulled a gun on  me. I was like, holy [BLEEP], and I just started kind of running around, and I was like, oh my god, they're going to kill us. They're actually going to kill us. It had finally sunk in that somebody was going to kill us, that that would be it, the end of my life. At like 14, somebody was going to kill me.
<v Sandra>我们站在那儿，一群女孩从我们身边经过，她们看着我们，我们也看着她们。当然，我们不假思索地脱口而出。你们看什么看？噢，你们有什么毛病吗？你们是混哪儿的？我们开始问她们属于哪个帮派之类的。我们开始对她们说些刻薄的脏话。然后她们掏出了一把枪，说，好啊，你们现在想说脏话是吧？那咱们就说个够。我当时惊慌失措。我是说，从来没有人拿枪指着我。我当时心想，天哪，我开始四处乱跑，我当时想，天哪，她们要杀了我们。她们真的要杀了我们。我终于意识到有人要杀我们，那将是我的生命尽头。我才14岁，竟然有人要杀我。

00:01:09.400 --> 00:02:05.280
<v Sandra>And it kind of flashed in my mind that here comes my mother finding me on the street corner with a bullet through my head and blood gushing everywhere, and oh my god. So everybody just kind of spread out, and I ran, and I lost my shoes, and my jacket, my purse. I lost everything. I was just like throwing things off me to make me lighter so I could run faster. And finally I jumped into a dumpster, and I stood there. And I must have stood there a good hour, hour and a half. And I just stood there and prayed and shaked, and tried to not make noise. And I heard them run past me, and run past me, and screaming "bitch" and looking for people. And I was there, and I was praying to God that they didn't open the dumpster and find me, because if they would have, I think I would have been dead.
<v Sandra>我脑子里闪过一个画面，我妈妈在街角发现了我，我头部中弹，血流满地，噢，天哪。所以大家四散奔逃，我跑着跑着，鞋子、夹克、钱包都掉了。我失去了一切。我当时只是把东西扔掉，好让自己跑得更快。最后我跳进了一个垃圾箱，缩在那里。我一定在那里站了整整一个小时到一个半小时。我就站在那儿，祈祷着，浑身发抖，尽量不发出声音。我听见她们从我身边跑过去，又跑回来，尖叫着骂着脏话，到处找人。我就在那儿，向上帝祈祷她们不要打开垃圾箱发现我，因为如果她们发现了，我想我就死定了。

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:25.230
<v Ira Glass>In movies and books, when somebody nearly dies it changes them. You know? They mend their ways. They treat people better. But in real life, it can go either way. Sandra waited and finally walked home alone, smelling like garbage, unrepentant.
<v Ira Glass>在电影和书中，当某人死里逃生时，这会改变他们。你知道吗？他们会改过自新。他们会更好地对待他人。但在现实生活中，结果可能会向任何方向发展。桑德拉等了一会儿，最后独自走回家，身上散发着垃圾的味道，毫无悔意。

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:42.180
<v Sandra>And my mother asked me, where's your shoes and your purse and your jacket and everything? And I told her that these girls were going to beat us up. I lied and I said that they started talking to us. They were gang-bangers. I told her like that, that they were gang-bangers, and they were looking for trouble. Knowing full well--
<v Sandra>我妈问我，你的鞋子、钱包、夹克还有其他东西呢？我告诉她，是那些女孩要打我们。我撒了谎，说她们先来招惹我们。她们是帮派成员。我就是这么告诉她的，说她们是帮派分子，是来找麻烦的。我心里很清楚——

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:44.060
<v Ira Glass>Yeah, knowing full well.
<v Ira Glass>是的，你心里很清楚。

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:53.350
<v Sandra>Knowing full well that I was the one that started it, and that I was gang-banging.
<v Sandra>我很清楚是我先挑起的争端，而且我才是那个混帮派的人。

00:02:53.350 --> 00:02:56.260
<v Ira Glass>But even after that you didn't quit the gang.
<v Ira Glass>但即使经历了那件事，你也没有退出帮派。

00:02:56.260 --> 00:03:12.710
<v Sandra>No. Why? A lot of different answers why. We didn't get hurt. We got to go back and tell our story. And none of us said that we hid. You know, none of us said we hid.
<v Sandra>没有。为什么？有很多不同的原因。我们没有受伤。我们回去讲述了我们的故事。我们谁也没有说我们躲起来了。你知道，我们谁也没说。

00:03:12.710 --> 00:03:13.500
<v Ira Glass>What did you say?
<v Ira Glass>你们说了什么？

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:16.450
<v Sandra>We said that we outran them.
<v Sandra>我们说我们跑得比她们快。

00:03:16.450 --> 00:03:19.050
<v Ira Glass>As if that's more heroic.
<v Ira Glass>好像那样显得更英勇似的。

00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:24.390
<v Sandra>It's better than saying I hid in the garbage can. That's cowardly.
<v Sandra>总比说我躲在垃圾桶里强。那太懦弱了。

00:03:24.390 --> 00:03:25.700
<v Ira Glass>But outrunning them is still just as fleeing.
<v Ira Glass>但跑得快也同样是逃跑。

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:35.590
<v Sandra>But they had a gun. It's fleeing, but it's honorable fleeing.
<v Sandra>但她们有枪。那是逃跑，但那是体面的逃跑。

00:03:35.590 --> 00:04:18.900
<v Ira Glass>Well, from WBEZ Chicago it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today on our program, stories of people who thought they were about to die, and how it affected them or didn't affect them. Act One, the day after Kevin slept on the spot Jesus was crucified. Act Two, the day Lawrence left the AIDS clinic for the big road trip. Act Three, the day Claudia asked her brother some questions. Act Four, the day Cheryl got the news. Act Five, the day Matthew saw Brigid. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>这里是芝加哥WBEZ电台的《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天我们的节目讲述了一些人以为自己即将死去的故事，以及这如何影响了他们，或者说，如何没有影响他们。第一幕，凯文睡在耶稣受难地点的第二天。第二幕，劳伦斯离开艾滋病诊所去远行。第三幕，克劳迪娅问她哥哥一些问题。第四幕，谢丽尔得到消息的那天。第五幕，马修见到布里吉德的那天。请继续收听。

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:40.800
<v Ira Glass>Act One. So what if you knew? What if you really knew the date and time of your own death? Well, Kevin Kelly spent most of his twenties wandering around Asia. He was a freelance photographer. And he found himself photographing a lot of religious ceremonies. He found himself drawn to religious ceremonies. He was confused about what he believed.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕。如果你知道会怎样？如果你真的知道自己死亡的日期和时间，会怎样？凯文·凯利二十多岁的大部分时间都在亚洲漫游。他是一名自由摄影师。他发现自己拍摄了很多宗教仪式。他发现自己被宗教仪式所吸引。他对自己所信仰的东西感到困惑。

00:04:40.800 --> 00:05:11.590
<v Kevin Kelly>I would get twisted and caught up, and these things were sort of in the background consuming me. Actually I found that I could think about little else for many, many months, that behind all that I was doing there was always this unresolved question of was God real? If he was real, then how could we ignore him? And if we were trying to not ignore him, what do we do? And if he was real, then what about these other things that people said about God?
<v Kevin Kelly>我会感到纠结和迷茫，这些事情在某种程度上在背景中消耗着我。实际上我发现，在过去的许多个月里，我几乎无法思考其他事情，我所做的一切背后总有一个悬而未决的问题：上帝是真的吗？如果他是真的，那我们怎么能忽视他？如果我们努力不忽视他，我们该做什么？如果他是真的，那人们关于上帝的其他说法又是怎么回事呢？

00:05:11.590 --> 00:05:21.230
<v Ira Glass>But all that changed. When he was 27, he came into Jerusalem. It was the weekend of both Easter and Passover, and the city was flooded with tourists.
<v Ira Glass>但一切都变了。当他27岁时，他来到了耶路撒冷。那是复活节和逾越节的周末，城里挤满了游客。

00:05:21.230 --> 00:05:58.720
<v Kevin Kelly>So I entered Jerusalem on Easter with the simple expectation that I was going to photograph yet another religious ceremony, another religious festival. And then for various reasons I got locked out of my hostel room. They had a curfew, and I didn't make it back in time. And I was in quite a fix because I was a stranger in this very strange town. When it happened, I didn't have enough money to stay elsewhere, nor did I even have knowledge of where to go.
<v Kevin Kelly>所以，我带着一个简单的期望在复活节进入了耶路撒冷，那就是去拍摄又一个宗教仪式，又一个宗教节日。然后由于各种原因，我被锁在了旅舍房间外。他们有宵禁，我没赶得回去。我陷入了困境，因为我是在这个非常陌生的城镇里的一个陌生人。事情发生时，我没有足够的钱去别处住宿，甚至不知道该去哪儿。

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:50.890
<v Kevin Kelly>So I wandered the old town of Jerusalem at night, which had been shuttered up and was like a time machine. It was if I had been transported back into the 15th century, because all the souvenir vendors were gone, and what was left were the labyrinthine paths of cobbled passageways. And I wandered around for a number of hours, and it was getting colder. Eventually I found myself at the one place that was still open, which was some of the churches. And particularly I finally settled into the Church of the Holy Sepulchre, which is called and viewed as the church built over the mound where Jesus Christ was crucified.
<v Kevin Kelly>所以我整夜在耶路撒冷的旧城徘徊，那里已经关门闭户，就像一台时间机器。仿佛我被传送回了15世纪，因为所有的纪念品小贩都走了，剩下的只有鹅卵石铺就的迷宫般的小巷。我徘徊了几个小时，天气越来越冷。最后我发现自己来到了唯一一个还开着的地方，那就是一些教堂。我最终定下心来，待在圣墓教堂，人们认为这座教堂是建立在耶稣基督受难的土墩之上的。

00:06:50.890 --> 00:07:34.280
<v Kevin Kelly>And I was getting very tired, and there weren't many people around. And so eventually I laid myself out on about the only flat area that was left, which was this marble slab underneath some pendants that had incense on them. And this was presumably the slab that commemorated the exact position of the crosses. So I slept there. I slept on the crucifixion spot that night because it was the only place. There was no place in the inn.
<v Kevin Kelly>我感到非常疲惫，周围没有几个人。于是我最终躺在仅剩的一块平坦区域上，那是挂着香炉的吊灯下的一块大理石板。这大概就是纪念十字架确切位置的石板。所以我睡在那儿。那天晚上我睡在受难地点，因为那是唯一的地方。客店里没有地方。

00:07:34.280 --> 00:08:09.290
<v Kevin Kelly>I slept there until early morning, when the activities started to increase, and people started coming in. And I went out and followed the crowd where it was going when they were going out to the tombs area in Jerusalem. And I went out, and there were some folding chairs set up in front of this tomb area. And as the sun was coming up on that Easter morning, I was staring at empty tombs.
<v Kevin Kelly>我一直睡到清晨，活动开始增多，人们开始进来。我走出去，跟着人群去了耶路撒冷的墓地区域。我走到外面，墓地前摆着一些折叠椅。当复活节的早晨太阳升起时，我正凝视着空坟墓。

00:08:09.290 --> 00:08:53.640
<v Kevin Kelly>And for a reason that I cannot comprehend, as I sat on that chair contemplating this view of the early sun morning coming into the empty tombs, all that I had been wrestling with for the past many, many years in thinking about religion sort of became resolved in my mind. And at that very moment, I believed that Jesus Christ has indeed risen from those tombs.
<v Kevin Kelly>出于某种我无法理解的原因，当我坐在那把椅子上凝视着清晨的阳光照进空坟墓时，过去多年来我在宗教思考上一直纠结的所有问题，在我的脑海中都得到了解决。就在那一刻，我相信耶稣基督确实从坟墓中复活了。

00:08:53.640 --> 00:09:24.170
<v Kevin Kelly>In an instant the tension of trying to figure things out was resolved, because now suddenly everything was figured out. It was as if you had been working on a problem for a long time, and suddenly the answer was there, and it was very clear that was the answer. And although there were many things that were still not clear to you, you were very certain that you were on the right path.
<v Kevin Kelly>那一瞬间，试图弄清事情真相的紧张感消失了，因为现在一切都突然明朗了。就像你长时间解决一个难题，答案突然就出现了，而且很清楚这就是答案。虽然还有很多事情你仍然不清楚，但你非常确定自己走在正确的道路上。

00:09:24.170 --> 00:10:04.940
<v Kevin Kelly>Having that realization that I believe that Jesus Christ had actually risen from those tombs did not settle 1,001 other things about what one was supposed to do with that, what I was supposed to do with that. Did that mean I was supposed to be a monk? Did that mean I was supposed to be an evangelist? Did  that mean that I had to immediately renounce all that I had and get into sackcloth and ashes and march out into the desert? All of that was left unopened, and that is in fact what occupied my mind as I went back to my hostel to lay down and think about, because I had no clue what it really meant to me ultimately.
<v Kevin Kelly>虽然我相信耶稣基督确实从坟墓中复活了，但这并没有解决关于一个人应该用这个事实做什么的其他一千零一件事，我应该用它做什么？那意味着我应该成为一名僧侣吗？那意味着我应该成为一名传教士吗？那意味着我必须立即放弃我所有的一切，披上麻布，撒上灰烬，走进沙漠吗？所有这些问题都没有答案，而这确实占据了我的思绪，当我回到旅舍躺下思考时，因为我完全不知道这对我最终意味着什么。

00:10:04.940 --> 00:10:42.690
<v Kevin Kelly>And that's what I was pondering when I sort of was laying there napping. And I wouldn't say it's a voice, but there was an idea that came into my mind that just would not go away. And that was that I should live as if I would die in six months, that I should really truly live. And that I could not tell for certain whether I would really die, but that either way I should live as if I was going to die. And so that was the assignment.
<v Kevin Kelly>当我躺在那儿小睡时，我就是在琢磨这些。我不会说这是一个声音，但我的脑海中产生了一个念头，而且挥之不去。那就是我应该像只剩六个月生命一样生活，我应该真正地生活。我无法确定我是否真的会死，但无论如何，我都应该像我就要死了一样生活。这就是那个使命。

00:10:42.690 --> 00:11:46.170
<v Kevin Kelly>I'm a pretty rational person. I'm pretty logical. And after thinking the thought that I should live as if I was going to die in six months, the first thought that comes to my head was, well that's pretty silly. You have no evidence whatsoever. I could live like I'm going to die in six months and not die at all. It would just be kind of an interesting exercise. But at the same time, it was equally probable that I might die in six months. It happened all the time. There was no guarantee that I wouldn't die. And so fairly quickly I decided that I could not settle that issue of whether I would really die or not, or just think that I was going to die in six months. And that in either case, the important thing was to live as if I really believed that I was going to die in six months. Which is what I set out to do.
<v Kevin Kelly>我是一个非常理性的人。我非常讲逻辑。在想到我应该像只剩六个月生命一样生活这个念头后，我脑海中出现的第一个想法是，好吧，这太蠢了。你没有任何证据。我可能像只剩六个月生命一样生活，却根本没死。这只会是一种有趣的练习。但与此同时，我确实可能在六个月内死去。这种情况时有发生。没人保证我不会死。于是我很快决定，我无法解决我是否真的会死的问题，或者只是认为我会在六个月内死去。无论哪种情况，重要的是像我真的相信我会死一样去生活。这就是我开始去做的事情。

00:11:46.170 --> 00:12:24.810
<v Kevin Kelly>The next couple days had a kind of joyous experience of saying, OK, what do I do for six months if I have only six months to live? And the answers to that surprised me as much as the assignment. Because after thinking that through and contemplating it, the conclusion that I came to was that what I wanted to do for six months was to go home and be ordinary, to go back to my parents, to help them take out the trash and trim the hedges and move furniture around, and to be with them.
<v Kevin Kelly>接下来的几天，有一种快乐的体验，那就是，“好吧，如果我只有六个月的生命，我该做什么？”这些问题的答案让我和这个使命一样感到惊讶。因为在深思熟虑之后，我得出的结论是，我想在那六个月里做的是回家，过平凡的生活，回到父母身边，帮他们倒垃圾、修剪树篱、搬动家具，和他们待在一起。

00:12:24.810 --> 00:12:54.710
<v Kevin Kelly>And I was really shocked by that, because I thought that with six months to live I would climb Mount Everest, or I would go scuba diving to the depths of the ocean, or get in a speedboat and see how fast I could go. But instead I wanted to go back home and be with my family for that time.
<v Kevin Kelly>我对此感到非常震惊，因为我原以为，如果只有六个月生命，我会去攀登珠穆朗玛峰，或者潜入深海，或者跳上一艘快艇看看能跑多快。但相反，我只想回家和我的家人共度那段时间。

00:12:54.710 --> 00:13:19.690
<v Kevin Kelly>I, of course, did not tell anybody my crazy idea. This is, in fact, the first time I'm really talking about it publicly, because it was a very scary and sort of alarming idea, and I never told anybody why I was coming home.
<v Kevin Kelly>当然，我没有把这个疯狂的想法告诉任何人。事实上，这是我第一次公开谈论它，因为它是一个非常可怕且令人不安的想法，我从未告诉过任何人我为什么回来。

00:13:19.690 --> 00:14:04.720
<v Kevin Kelly>I got back to where my parents live in New Jersey, and things were unbelievably ordinary. And yet I found myself relishing the ordinariness and finding it in some ways as exotic as anything that I had traveled to see. And so I was involved in-- I helped around the house. I dug up shrubs. I worked on a deck. I moved furniture, washed dishes, and I was intending to kind of spend my last remaining six months at home getting to know my parents better, and myself hopefully.
<v Kevin Kelly>我回到了父母居住的新泽西州，一切都不可思议地平凡。然而，我发现自己非常享受这种平凡，并觉得它在某种程度上和我去过任何地方一样充满异国情调。所以我参与其中——我在家里帮忙。我挖灌木。我修整露台。我搬动家具、洗碗，我打算把剩下的六个月花在家里，更好地了解我的父母，也希望更好地了解自己。

00:14:04.720 --> 00:14:33.920
<v Kevin Kelly>But about three months into that, my travel urges I guess got the better of me. And what I was most concerned about is I wanted to see my brothers and sisters. I had four brothers and sisters, and they were scattered all across the country. And so I felt very strongly that I wanted to see them before I died. And I got the idea that the way to see them was to ride my bicycle across the country and visit them on bicycle.
<v Kevin Kelly>但在那之后大约三个月，我的旅行欲望我想还是战胜了我。我最关心的是我想见我的兄弟姐妹。我有四个兄弟姐妹，他们散居在全国各地。所以我强烈地感到，在去世前我想见见他们。我产生了一个想法，去见他们的方法就是骑自行车穿越全国，骑自行车拜访他们。

00:14:33.920 --> 00:15:28.620
<v Kevin Kelly>But before I did that, I made up a will to dispose of the little things that I had. And I had some money left over, and one of the things that I did with that money was I went to the bank and got some cashier's checks for like $500 and $1,000. And I mailed the money to various people anonymously as gifts. And I think giving away those thousands of dollars was the first true act of charity I had ever done, because there was absolutely no way for any kind of gratitude or elevated feelings to come back to me, because people had no idea who had sent them that money.
<v Kevin Kelly>但在我这样做之前，我立了一份遗嘱，处理我所拥有的一点点东西。我剩下了一些钱，我用这些钱做的事情之一就是去银行买了一些面值500美元和1000美元的现金支票。我匿名把这些钱作为礼物寄给了不同的人。我认为捐出那几千美元是我做过的第一次真正的慈善行为，因为根本不可能有任何感激或崇高的感觉回馈给我，因为人们根本不知道是谁寄给他们那笔钱的。

00:15:28.620 --> 00:16:06.770
<v Kevin Kelly>It was really remarkable to see the consequences of getting an anonymous gift like that, because when you get a check for $1,000 in the mail, you immediately become suspicious of all your friends of having given it to you. And so there's this sort of like the suspicion of charity, suspicion of goodness, that starts to infect the people that are around you. And you look at someone, you think, hmm, I wonder if he gave me that $1,000?
<v Kevin Kelly>看到收到这种匿名礼物的结果真的非常了不起，因为当你收到一张1000美元的支票时，你立刻会怀疑是不是你所有的朋友给你的。所以这种对慈善的怀疑，对善意的怀疑，开始感染你周围的人。你看着某人，心想，嗯，不知道是不是他给了我那1000美元？

00:16:06.770 --> 00:16:29.750
<v Kevin Kelly>I had enough money left over to basically pay for food and whatnot on my bicycle journey across America. And the path that I had to visit all my brothers and sisters was not a direct route of going from San Francisco to New York. I actually had to go up to Idaho and back down to Texas, and then back up through Indiana. So it was a 5,000 mile trip.
<v Kevin Kelly>我剩下的钱基本上够我骑自行车横穿美国的食物开销。为了拜访我所有的兄弟姐妹，路线不是从旧金山到纽约的直达路线。我实际上不得不绕道爱达荷州，再回到德克萨斯州，然后再绕道印第安纳州。所以这是一次5000英里的旅程。

00:16:29.750 --> 00:17:12.720
<v Kevin Kelly>The day, which coincidentally was exactly six months from when I had this assignment, was October 31, was Halloween. And so the plan would be that I would ride back home so that I would come back to die on the day after Halloween.
<v Kevin Kelly>那一天，巧合的是距离我接到这个使命整整六个月，是10月31日，万圣节。所以计划是，我会骑车回家，这样我就会在万圣节的第二天死掉。

00:17:12.720 --> 00:18:10.180
<v Kevin Kelly>I think there are a lot of people who have trouble staying in the present. There are some people who like to slip into the past as a means to perhaps fantasize or escape, and they find that the past is the place that they retreat to. And I often retreat to the future. I was not a person who planned or had a career staged out, or who had a particular woman he wanted to marry someday, or some vision of a house. The future that I found so hard to give up was a much more insidious type. It was that of-- I like to buy this record because in the future I want to hear the song again and again. Or I will read this book, and there's some cool ideas in it because someday I may write an article about this, and it's kind of good to know that.
<v Kevin Kelly>我想有很多人很难活在当下。有些人喜欢沉浸在过去，作为幻想或逃避的一种手段，他们发现过去是他们退守的地方。而我经常退守到未来。我不是那种会做计划、有事业规划、或者有某个想在某天娶的女人、或者对房产有某种愿景的人。我发现很难放弃的未来是一种更阴险的类型。那就是——我买这张唱片是因为在未来我想一遍又一遍地听这首歌。或者我会读这本书，里面有一些很酷的想法，因为总有一天我可能会写一篇关于这个的文章，知道这一点感觉挺好的。

00:18:10.180 --> 00:18:45.060
<v Kevin Kelly>There was a sense in which my entire life was shifted to the future, and the thought of sort of doing something now for the enjoyment or the pleasures or the principle of the function of just right now, without any sense at all that it would ever be used again or that it could ever be brought forward, was extremely difficult and disconcerting. And I fought it day by day, and tooth by tooth.
<v Kevin Kelly>在某种意义上，我的整个人生都转移到了未来，而那种为了此时此刻的乐趣、快乐或纯粹的功能原则去做某事，完全没有任何它会被再次使用或可能被带到未来的念头，这极其困难且令人不安。我一天天地抗争，咬牙坚持。

00:18:45.060 --> 00:19:51.030
<v Kevin Kelly>One of the ways I dealt with this was that I was actually able by the last weeks to not think about my life beyond Halloween. There was a way in which I had just-- each time a thought came up about something that was beyond this horizon, I just said, nope, can't think about, it doesn't work. We have to dwell in the present. And at the same time I was doing that-- and I was able to do that-- I also decided that it was entirely unnatural and inhumane way to live, and that having a future is part of what being human is about, and that when you take away the future for humans, you take away a lot of their humanness. And that it's not actually a very good thing to live entirely in the present, that one needs to have a past, and one needs to have a future to be fully human.
<v Kevin Kelly>我处理这个问题的方法之一是，在最后几周，我实际上能够不去想万圣节之后的生活。每当有一个关于超出这个范围的想法出现时，我就会说，不，不能想，那行不通。我们必须活在当下。同时我也决定，这是一种极其不自然和非人道的生活方式，拥有未来是身为人类的一部分，当你夺走人类的未来时，你也夺走了他们很大一部分人性。而完全活在当下实际上并不是一件好事，一个人需要有过去，也需要有未来才能成为一个完整的人。

00:19:51.030 --> 00:20:25.580
<v Ira Glass>So he bicycled across the country, and as he did he found himself increasingly obsessed with death, with dying. He was making drawings and writing haikus along the way, and as he went across the country, they became more and more dark, more and more preoccupied with death. And as he traveled across the country, the other thing is that he always had to find a place to stay every night. And at first he just tried what he had done in Jerusalem. That is, he would just find a church, and he would camp out on the church lawn. But people got upset at that.
<v Ira Glass>所以他骑车横跨了全国，随着旅程的推进，他发现自己越来越痴迷于死亡，痴迷于死去。一路上他在画画和写俳句，随着他横跨全国，这些内容变得越来越阴暗，越来越沉溺于死亡。在他横跨全国的过程中，另一件事是他必须每天晚上找个地方住。起初他只是尝试他在耶路撒冷做过的事。也就是说，他会找个教堂，在教堂草坪上露营。但人们对此很不高兴。

00:20:25.580 --> 00:20:51.340
<v Ira Glass>And so what he decided to do instead is that at each town that he would come into, he would find some quiet residential street. And he'd go up to a house, and he'd say hi, this is who I am. I'm bicycling across the country. I've just eaten dinner, he would say so they wouldn't feel obligated to feed him. Can I camp out in back? And invariably they would say yes, and invariably they would also at some point invite him in for coffee.
<v Ira Glass>所以他决定这样做，在每个城镇，他都会找一条安静的住宅街道。他会走到一户人家，说你好，我是谁谁谁。我正在骑车横跨全国。我已经吃过晚饭了，他说，这样他们就不会觉得有义务喂他。我能在后院扎营吗？他们总是会说可以，而且总是会在某个时候邀请他进去喝杯咖啡。

00:20:51.340 --> 00:21:41.620
<v Kevin Kelly>So I would spend my evenings with entirely ordinary people, and my job at that point was to tell them my story. It was to tell them how great a time I was having, because if I wasn't having a great time they were really disappointed, because I was riding for them. I was vicariously doing what they had always wanted their lives to do, and so the more I enjoyed my time, and the more in the present I was living, the more they enjoyed it, and the more it uplifted them. And so that was basically my job.
<v Kevin Kelly>所以我会在晚上和完全普通的人待在一起，我那时的任务就是给他们讲我的故事。讲我过得有多开心，因为如果我过得不开心，他们会非常失望，因为我是替他们骑行的。我替代性地做了他们一直想要生活做的事，所以正如我享受我的时光，正如我生活在当下，他们就越享受，这也就越提升他们。所以那基本上就是我的工作。

00:21:41.620 --> 00:23:05.580
<v Kevin Kelly>It was a journey that began at the tomb of Jesus, and as I set off to my own presumed death, I did indeed think about Jesus Christ. We have the history in the Gospels of Jesus' torment in his soul as he approached what he knew of his anointed time to die. So it was again that very harsh information of knowing when you're going to die. And Jesus' soul was in great turmoil and pain because of knowing that. And I think I did experience some of that, not because I had the same weight. That was just my own life. But Jesus prayed that this burden be lifted, and there were days when I did pray that, that if I didn't have to die, I really would rather not.
<v Kevin Kelly>那是一段始于耶稣之墓的旅程，当我踏上我预想的死亡之路时，我确实想到了耶稣基督。我们在福音书中读到耶稣在接近他所知的受膏死期时，灵魂受到的折磨。这又是那种知道自己何时会死的残酷信息。耶稣的灵魂因为知道这一点而陷入了巨大的动荡和痛苦。我想我确实经历了一些那种痛苦，不是因为我有同样的重担。那只是我自己的生活。但耶稣祈求这重担能够免去，确实有几天我祈祷过，如果我不必死，我真的宁愿不死。

00:23:05.580 --> 00:23:38.520
<v Kevin Kelly>By late fall I was pedaling through the Appalachians, and it was getting colder and colder, and my hands were freezing on the bicycles, and there was ice on my tents in the morning when I got up. And as each day went on, I was coming closer and closer to terrain that I was familiar with and that felt like home. And I was riding into New Jersey, and I was elated. I was elated that I had accomplished this long journey, and I was elated that I was home to see my parents.
<v Kevin Kelly>到了深秋，我正在穿越阿巴拉契亚山脉，天气越来越冷，我在自行车上的双手冻僵了，每天早上起来帐篷上都有冰。随着每一天过去，我越来越接近我熟悉的、感觉像家的地形。我骑进了新泽西州，我欣喜若狂。我欣喜若狂是因为我完成了这段漫长的旅程，我欣喜若狂是因为我回家见到了我的父母。

00:23:38.520 --> 00:24:07.950
<v Kevin Kelly>And I came into their house on Halloween day, and I was so filled with ideas and things and emotions that I didn't really say very much. And again, I couldn't say very much. I think we had a wonderful dinner. They were, of course, glad to see me, because they hadn't seen me in a long time. They knew I was coming back, and we had a wonderful dinner. We had baskets of candy which I gave out to the kids.
<v Kevin Kelly>万圣节那天我回到家，我充满了想法、事物和情绪，以至于我没有说太多话。再次强调，我无法说太多。我想我们吃了一顿很棒的晚餐。他们当然很高兴见到我，因为他们很久没见我了。他们知道我会回来，我们吃了一顿美妙的晚餐。我们有几篮糖果，我发给了孩子们。

00:24:07.950 --> 00:24:41.200
<v Kevin Kelly>And we had a discussion that night which was about nothing in particular. It was not about the future. It was just about I think talking about our family, and my brothers and sisters, and I was telling them all that I had learned about them. And so it was a very together and, again, not a very dramatic evening, but just a pleasant one, the kind of one that you might have a memory about as you were dying. It was not a special evening, but just an ordinary evening.
<v Kevin Kelly>那天晚上我们进行了一场谈话，谈的内容没什么特别的。不是关于未来。我想只是谈谈我们的家庭，我的兄弟姐妹，我告诉他们我所了解的所有关于他们的事。所以这是一个非常团结，再次强调，不是一个非常戏剧性的夜晚，而是一个愉快的夜晚，那种你临终时可能会回忆起的夜晚。这不是一个特别的夜晚，只是一个平凡的夜晚。

00:24:41.200 --> 00:25:14.190
<v Kevin Kelly>And I went to bed that night, which was a very difficult thing to do, because I was fully prepared at that point never to wake up again. I had been praying. I had gotten everything arranged. And at that point I'd fully gone through in my own mind, my own soul, all the things that I might have regretted, and I had righted as many of those as I thought I could through letters. And I was prepared as much as anybody could be prepared to die.
<v Kevin Kelly>那天晚上我上床睡觉了，这非常困难，因为在那一刻我已做好了准备永远不再醒来。我一直在祈祷。我把一切都安排好了。那时我在自己的脑海中、在自己的灵魂深处，把所有我可能后悔的事情都回顾了一遍，通过信件我尽可能地纠正了它们。我做好了任何能做准备的人所能做好的死亡准备。

00:25:14.190 --> 00:25:32.820
<v Kevin Kelly>So I went to bed while the kids were still ringing the doorbells. And I went to sleep, because I was very tired after that long trip. And I didn't know what was going to happen the next day. I thought that I had done all that I could.
<v Kevin Kelly>所以当孩子们还在按门铃时，我就上床睡觉了。我睡着了，因为那次长途跋涉后我非常疲惫。我不知道第二天会发生什么。我想我已经尽了我所能做的一切。

00:25:32.820 --> 00:26:58.900
<v Kevin Kelly>And the next morning I woke up. And the next morning I woke up, and it was if-- the next morning I woke up, and it was if I had my entire life again. The next morning I woke up, and I had my entire life again. I had my future again. There was nothing special about the day. It was another ordinary day. I was reborn into ordinariness, but what more could one ask for?
<v Kevin Kelly>第二天早上我醒了。第二天早上我醒了，就好像——第二天早上我醒了，就好像我又拥有了整个人生。第二天早上我醒了，我又拥有了整个人生。我又拥有了未来。这一天没有什么特别的。这是又一个平凡的一天。我重生于平凡，但一个人还能要求什么呢？

00:26:58.900 --> 00:27:59.230
<v Ira Glass>Nearly two decades after that happened, Kevin Kelly is now the executive editor of a magazine about the future, Wired magazine.
<v Ira Glass>在那件事发生近二十年后，凯文·凯利现在是一家关于未来杂志《连线》杂志的执行编辑。

00:27:59.230 --> 00:29:08.600
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, more people who nearly died and what they made of it, in a minute when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，更多濒临死亡的人以及他们的感悟，一会儿节目继续。

00:29:08.600 --> 00:29:31.040
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our program, of course, we choose a theme, invite a variety of writers and performers and documentary producers to tackle that theme. Today's program, Should Have Been Dead, stories of people who thought they were going to die and what they learned or did not learn from the experience.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。每周在我们的节目中，当然，我们会选择一个主题，邀请各种作家、表演者和纪录片制作人来探讨这个主题。今天的节目，《本该死掉的人》，讲述了一些人以为自己即将死去，以及他们从这段经历中学到了什么，或者没学到什么。

00:29:31.040 --> 00:29:56.660
<v Ira Glass>We're at Act Two of our program, Maybe Six Months. Well, Lawrence Steger is a filmmaker and a performance artist here in Chicago, and I told him about Kevin Kelly's story. And it turned out that, like Kevin Kelly, at the moment that Lawrence thought he'd glimpsed the possible end of his life, Lawrence also set out on a big trip.
<v Ira Glass>我们进入了节目的第二幕，《或许六个月》。劳伦斯·斯蒂格是芝加哥的一名电影制作人和表演艺术家，我告诉了他凯文·凯利的故事。结果发现，就像凯文·凯利一样，在劳伦斯以为自己瞥见了生命尽头的瞬间，劳伦斯也开始了一次大旅行。

00:29:56.660 --> 00:30:12.160
<v Lawrence Steger>Title-- Road. Treatment-- it's shot entirely on video, mostly handheld, shaky, out of focus, bad color, overblown color actually.
<v Lawrence Steger>片名——《公路》。处理——它完全用视频拍摄，大多手持，摇晃，失焦，颜色糟糕，实际上是过度饱和的颜色。

00:30:12.160 --> 00:30:15.450
<v Lawrence Steger>Can I get this microphone adjusted a little so I don't have to lean over so much?
<v Lawrence Steger>能把这个麦克风调一下吗，这样我就不用弯腰那么多了？

00:30:15.450 --> 00:30:18.190
<v Ira Glass>Yeah, sure.
<v Ira Glass>好的，没问题。

00:30:18.190 --> 00:30:22.430
