00:32:52.400 --> 00:33:21.210
<v Lawrence Steger>Luke thinks he's sounding like a short story assignment in a creative writing class, or worse, trapped inside an artsy novel. Luke imagines himself in a television dramatization of himself. Camera pulls back from behind Luke's head, sort of on a mini-crane. Camera floats, hovers over the back of Luke's head. The ceiling of the car must be incredibly high, he thinks to himself.
<v Lawrence Steger>剧情不断地被卢克那种冷漠的举止所破坏。他显得很疏离。引文：“我不知道我感觉如何。我感到有点难过，有点生气。我猜是空白，但我还好。”引文结束。

00:33:21.210 --> 00:34:08.810
<v Lawrence Steger>Bill pulls into the closest parking spot in the clinic, blows out the last of the one-hit, and as he's knocking the brass pipe into the ashtray, turns to Luke with that slightly watery look in his eyes from too much intake. Luke takes it as one of those Care Bear looks that he's experienced before from Bill. There's a moment of anger flashing in Luke when he registers Bill's look. When Bill asks him, "What are you thinking about?" Luke responds, "Who's thinking? Nothing." I hate thinking I'm in a novel, he thinks to himself.
<v Lawrence Steger>卢克觉得听起来像创意写作课上的短篇小说作业，或者更糟，被困在了一本艺术化的小说里。卢克想象自己出现在自己生活的电视连续剧里。摄影机在小型起重机上，从卢克头后方拉远。摄影机浮动，盘旋在卢克后脑勺上方。他心想，车顶一定高得不可思议。

00:34:08.810 --> 00:34:27.530
<v Lawrence Steger>Cut to interior of the clinic, the reception area. Can we change this sound bed here? Great. Take it down just it's a little-- great.
<v Lawrence Steger>比尔把车停在诊所最近的停车位，呼出最后一口烟雾，当他把黄铜烟斗敲向烟灰缸时，他转头看向卢克，眼神里带着一丝因吸食过量而产生的湿润。卢克将其视为他以前从比尔那里感受到的那种“爱心熊”式的眼神。当卢克注意到比尔的目光时，心中闪过一丝愤怒。当比尔问他：“你在想什么？”卢克回答道：“谁在想？什么也没想。”他心里暗想，我讨厌那种觉得自己身处小说中的感觉。

00:34:27.530 --> 00:35:08.580
<v Lawrence Steger>The nurse assigned to Luke's anonymous number is a black drag queen named Stephanie who wears a full nurse's outfit complete with a little paper hat that sits atop of her freshly coiffed hairdo. She's the only one in the clinic who wears a real uniform. Stephanie has the longest fingernails that Luke has ever seen on anyone. Luke thinks briefly about how the fingernails keep on growing even after a person dies, but he pushes that thought away with his fingers to his forehead, wonders why he's thinking about that. It's that novel thing again.
<v Lawrence Steger>转场至诊所内部，接待区。我们能改变这里的音效吗？很好。压低一点——很好。

00:35:08.580 --> 00:35:31.410
<v Lawrence Steger>Stephanie, the drag queen nurse, walks Luke back to the small cubicles that the tests are administered in, and then used to relay the results. Luke's narrator imagines how many people have been in these cubicles and what they would look like if they were all piled on top of one another, piles of tested bodies.
<v Lawrence Steger>负责卢克匿名编号的护士是一位黑人变装皇后，名叫斯蒂芬妮，她穿着一套完整的护士装，头上顶着一顶小纸帽，戴在她精心打理的发型上。她是诊所里唯一穿正规制服的人。斯蒂芬妮有着卢克见过任何人当中最长的指甲。卢克简短地想到了指甲在人死后还会继续生长，但他用手指按住额头把这个想法推开，纳闷自己为什么要那样想。又是那种小说情节。

00:35:31.410 --> 00:35:48.170
<v Lawrence Steger>Cut to Stephanie closing the hollow core door. It makes that hollow core door sound. Do you have that on a cart? [DOOR CLOSES] Perfect. Maybe a shot from a security camera that shows all of the cubicles in the clinic.
<v Lawrence Steger>变装皇后护士斯蒂芬妮带着卢克走进进行测试的小隔间，那里也用于转达结果。卢克的旁白想象着有多少人进过这些隔间，如果把他们全都堆在一起会是什么样子，一堆接受过测试的尸体。

00:35:48.170 --> 00:36:08.200
<v Lawrence Steger>Luke imagines himself in a George Tooker painting that was reproduced in his sixth grade reader. He wonders what his sixth grade teacher would think of Stephanie. He wonders if his sixth grade teacher was ever tested. He imagines her body in the pileup of bodies who have come to the clinic.
<v Lawrence Steger>转场至斯蒂芬妮关上空心门。发出那种空心门的声音。你有那个音效吗？[门关上]完美。或许从安全摄像头的角度拍一张显示诊所里所有隔间的镜头。

00:36:08.200 --> 00:36:37.640
<v Lawrence Steger>Stephanie's been saying something, and Luke has to blink his eyes again to refocus. He explains to Stephanie that he's been expecting this result, that he's experienced a large share of AIDS cared for and likewise buried lots of his friends, but it doesn't seem to come as a surprise. Stephanie says, "You can cry or hold my hand. I just want you to sit for a moment and let it sink in." Luke thinks, whatever.
<v Lawrence Steger>卢克想象自己置身于六年级课本中转载的那幅乔治·塔克的画里。他想知道他的六年级老师会怎么看斯蒂芬妮。他想知道他的六年级老师是否做过测试。他想象着她的身体在来到诊所的尸体堆里。

00:36:37.640 --> 00:37:13.110
<v Lawrence Steger>Cut to Bill in waiting room, flipping through People's "The Year in Pictures." Cut back to close-up of Luke, forehead wrinkled. He thinks his narrator wants him to get out of the cubicle. He waits for Stephanie to finish her spiel, thanks her, and shakes her hand, getting a slight scrape from one of the fingernails. Close-up on Luke's hand, new scratch. The walls seem to pulsate as Luke walks down the hallway to the reception area. He tries to be as blank as possible to Bill.
<v Lawrence Steger>斯蒂芬妮一直在说着什么，卢克不得不再次眨眼聚焦。他向斯蒂芬妮解释说他一直预料到这个结果，他经历过照顾许多艾滋病患者，也埋葬过许多朋友，但这似乎并不让他感到惊讶。斯蒂芬妮说：“你可以哭，或者握住我的手。我只是想让你坐一会儿，让这个事实沉淀一下。”卢克心想，随它去吧。

00:37:13.110 --> 00:37:29.570
<v Lawrence Steger>I'm not sure about this final section. I know that we talked about it ending on the highway with the car being surrounded by bikers on their way to the Sturgis bikers rally, but now I like the idea of it ending on the highway entrance ramp. OK.
<v Lawrence Steger>转场至候诊室里的比尔，翻看着《人物》杂志的《年度图片》。转场回卢克的特写，眉头紧锁。他觉得他的旁白想让他离开隔间。他等着斯蒂芬妮说完她的套话，感谢她，并与她握手，被其中一个指甲轻轻划伤。卢克的手部特写，新伤痕。当卢克沿着走廊走向接待区时，墙壁似乎在搏动。他尽量向比尔表现得空白一些。

00:37:29.570 --> 00:38:01.280
<v Lawrence Steger>Cut to interior of car pulling out of parking lot. Luke keeps looking straight ahead as he murmurs, "I'm positive." Long slow pan from the back of Luke's head to the back of Bill's. There's no reaction in either of their faces, or better, the profiles of their faces. This is the longest shot. They don't look at each other.
<v Lawrence Steger>我不确定最后这一部分。我知道我们谈过以公路为结尾，车子被一群正前往斯特吉斯摩托车集会的骑手包围，但现在我喜欢以高速公路入口匝道为结尾的想法。好的。

00:38:01.280 --> 00:38:32.900
<v Lawrence Steger>Perhaps this scene would be shot in blue screen with the camera in the backseat, and the sky surrounding the two heads of Bill and Luke having that old scratchy 16 millimeter time lapse exposure so the clouds seem to be moving at a rapid pace. Flickers, flips back and forth between real sky and blue screen backdrop. Voice comes up on a car radio, try not to think of the future. Just live in the present moment, something like that. You got that? It comes on to the radio.
<v Lawrence Steger>转场至离开停车场的汽车内部。卢克直视前方，喃喃自语道：“我呈阳性。”从卢克的后脑勺慢慢平移到比尔的后脑勺。他们两人的脸上都没有表情，或者更好的是，他们的侧脸轮廓。这是最长的镜头。他们没有看对方。

00:38:32.900 --> 00:38:53.190
<v Kevin Kelly>I also decided that it was entirely unnatural and inhumane way to live, and that having a future is part of what being human is about. And that when you take away the future for humans, you take away a lot of their humanness. And that it's not actually a very good thing to live entirely in the present, that one needs to have a past.
<v Kevin Kelly>或许这个场景可以用蓝幕拍摄，摄像机放在后座，包围比尔和卢克头部的天空有着那种古老的、带有划痕的16毫米延时曝光，所以云朵似乎在快速移动。闪烁，在真实天空和蓝幕背景之间翻转。收音机里传出声音，尽量不去想未来。就活在当下，诸如此类的。你有那个吗？它在收音机里响起来。

00:38:53.190 --> 00:39:24.410
<v Lawrence Steger>Luke comments to Bill, "Live entirely in the present, huh?" Bill drives and looks out the corner of his right eye to see what position Luke is holding his head in. Luke looks outside passenger window, and every once in a while turns to glance at Bill. Long pause. There's dead air.
<v Lawrence Steger>我还决定，这是一种极其不自然和非人道的生活方式，拥有未来是身为人类的一部分。当你夺走人类的未来时，你也夺走了他们很大一部分人性。而完全活在当下实际上并不是一件好事，一个人需要有过去。

00:39:24.410 --> 00:40:46.980
<v Lawrence Steger>Cut to Luke's point of view. Car is pulling on to entrance ramp of highway. Luke sees hitchhiker with a sign that he scans for any remote meaning to the narrative. Luke sees himself outside of his own story. He can't read the hitchhiker's sign. He knows that he's on a long, silent journey. He leans over to turn off the radio. Cut to black.
<v Lawrence Steger>卢克对比尔评论道：“活在当下，是吧？”比尔开着车，用右眼余光瞥了一眼，看卢克把头保持在什么位置。卢克看着乘客窗外，偶尔转过头瞥一眼比尔。长暂停。是一段死寂。

00:40:46.980 --> 00:40:48.710
<v Ira Glass>Act Three.
<v Ira Glass>转场至卢克的视角。汽车正驶入高速公路入口匝道。卢克看到一个拿着牌子的搭车人，他扫描着寻找任何与叙事相关的遥远意义。卢克看到自己置身于自己的故事之外。他读不懂搭车人的牌子。他知道他踏上了一段漫长、沉默的旅程。他俯身关掉收音机。转场至黑屏。

00:40:48.710 --> 00:40:53.470
<v Claudia Perez>This is is a show Claudia Perez is doing on my brother Javier.
<v Claudia Perez>第三幕。

00:40:53.470 --> 00:40:55.340
<v Javier>[UNINTELLIGIBLE]
<v Javier>这是克劳迪娅·佩雷斯正在制作的一档关于我哥哥哈维尔的节目。

00:40:55.340 --> 00:40:58.490
<v Claudia Perez>Shut up. No wonder your friends can't listen to this.
<v Claudia Perez>[听不清]

00:40:58.490 --> 00:41:04.870
<v Javier>[UNINTELLIGIBLE]
<v Javier>闭嘴。难怪你的朋友听不下去这个。

00:41:04.870 --> 00:41:07.860
<v Claudia Perez>Did you ever have any thoughts like oh, I want to die?
<v Claudia Perez>[听不清]

00:41:07.860 --> 00:41:08.360
<v Javier>[UNINTELLIGIBLE]
<v Javier>你有过任何想法，比如噢，我想死吗？

00:41:08.360 --> 00:41:12.750
<v Claudia Perez>No, I don't got to-- have you ever imagined your death, like--
<v Claudia Perez>[听不清]

00:41:12.750 --> 00:41:13.700
<v Javier>[INAUDIBLE]
<v Javier>不，我没必要——你有没有想象过你的死亡，比如——

00:41:13.700 --> 00:41:22.250
<v Claudia Perez>[INAUDIBLE] In April of 1996, how did your day begin the day that you got shot?
<v Claudia Perez>[听不清]

00:41:22.250 --> 00:41:33.850
<v Javier>[BLEEP] [UNINTELLIGIBLE]
<v Javier>[听不清] 在1996年4月，你中枪那天你是怎么开始的？

00:41:33.850 --> 00:41:46.820
<v Ira Glass>That's a recording of Claudia Perez, who's 19 years old, an occasional contributor to this program, interviewing her 21-year-old brother. And her brother had an experience where he and his family thought he might die.
<v Ira Glass>[哔声] [听不清]

00:41:46.820 --> 00:41:50.110
<v Ira Glass>And Claudia, what is on this tape?
<v Ira Glass>那是19岁、偶尔为本节目投稿的克劳迪娅·佩雷斯的录音，她在采访她21岁的哥哥。她的哥哥经历过一次他自己和家人都以为他会死的事件。

00:41:50.110 --> 00:42:02.220
<v Claudia Perez>He was being a big brat. He's laying down on the bed, and I'm sitting on his wheelchair, and I'm interviewing him. And he keeps trying to grab the microphone from me.
<v Claudia Perez>克劳迪娅，这张磁带里是什么内容？

00:42:02.220 --> 00:42:08.630
<v Claudia Perez>Tell me what happened, like what you see, what you thought of that moment when you got shot? What happened?
<v Claudia Perez>他像个大顽童一样。他躺在床上，我坐在他的轮椅上采访他。他一直想从我手里抢麦克风。

00:42:08.630 --> 00:42:14.550
<v Javier>What happened? Well, I got a hole in my body, and I was bleeding.
<v Javier>告诉我发生了什么，比如你看到的，你中枪那一刻你在想什么？发生了什么？

00:42:14.550 --> 00:42:19.700
<v Claudia Perez>That is so stupid. Let go of me.
<v Claudia Perez>发生了什么？好吧，我的身体上出了个洞，我在流血。

00:42:19.700 --> 00:42:21.850
<v Ira Glass>So how did you bring him around? What did you do?
<v Ira Glass>那太愚蠢了。放开我。

00:42:21.850 --> 00:42:28.050
<v Claudia Perez>I had to open up the story. I had to take him back so he could remember.
<v Claudia Perez>你是怎么引导他谈论的？你做了什么？

00:42:28.050 --> 00:42:30.980
<v Ira Glass>Why don't you take us back? What exactly happened to him?
<v Ira Glass>我不得不开启这个话题。我不得不带他回到过去，这样他才能记起来。

00:42:30.980 --> 00:42:57.860
<v Claudia Perez>Well, it was April 2 or April 6 of '96, and he had come to the house in the morning. And he had bought some gym shoes, and he was showing off, ha ha, look, I got some gym shoes. And you're not supposed to buy gym shoes because that's not fair, because you're not buying stuff for my little brother. And I was like, you're being greedy. Something's going to happen to you for being greedy.
<v Claudia Perez>为什么不带我们回到过去？他到底发生了什么？

00:42:57.860 --> 00:43:08.960
<v Claudia Perez>And I remember that I felt weird that day. You know how they say women have this kind of sense? I don't know, but I felt weird.
<v Claudia Perez>好吧，那是96年4月2日或6日，他早上到过家里。他买了一些运动鞋，正在炫耀，哈哈，看，我有新运动鞋了。你不应该买运动鞋，因为那不公平，因为你没给我弟弟买东西。我就说，你太贪心了。你会因为贪心而遭遇报应的。

00:43:08.960 --> 00:43:45.790
<v Javier>So it was quick. It happened quick. Go for the gun quick, and then turn around and take off. But as soon as I turned around, that's when I got shot. Then everything happened so fast. I seen like a white light. Everybody says they seen a light, but it is weird, because you see like a little light. Everything's like in slow motion. I was trying to-- like oh, this is a dream. I'm going to go back to sleep. I'm going to wake up, and then I'm going to be home in bed next to my wife and kids. And this is only a bad dream. I can shake it off.
<v Javier>我记得那天我感觉很奇怪。你知道人们常说女人有这种直觉吗？我不知道，但我感觉很奇怪。

00:43:45.790 --> 00:43:56.580
<v Claudia Perez>One of his friends, they were going to go party at a bar, and they went towards the back of the gangway. They were over there in the back of the gangway, and--
<v Claudia Perez>事情发生得很快。去拿枪很快，然后转身逃跑。但就在我转身的时候，我就中枪了。一切发生得太快了。我看到像是一道白光。每个人都说他们看到了光，但这很奇怪，因为你确实看到了一点光。一切都像是慢动作。我试图——就像噢，这是一个梦。我要回去睡觉。我要醒来，然后我要在家里和妻子孩子躺在床上。这只是一个噩梦。我可以把它甩掉。

00:43:56.580 --> 00:43:57.530
<v Ira Glass>The game way is?
<v Ira Glass>他的一个朋友，他们本来要去酒吧聚会，他们走向小巷的后方。他们在那里，在小巷的后方，然后——

00:43:57.530 --> 00:43:58.730
<v Claudia Perez>A house. Of a house.
<v Claudia Perez>小巷是？

00:43:58.730 --> 00:44:00.080
<v Ira Glass>OK. Oh, the gangway.
<v Ira Glass>一所房子。房子的。

00:44:00.080 --> 00:44:08.850
<v Claudia Perez>Yeah. And they were just all talking there, and the guy stuck out the gun to my brother, and my brother tried to take it from his hand.
<v Claudia Perez>好的。噢，小巷。

00:44:08.850 --> 00:44:11.300
<v Ira Glass>Wait, now this guy came out of where? Was he in a car? Was he walking?
<v Ira Glass>是的。他们都在那里谈话，那个人掏枪指向我哥哥，我哥哥试图把它从他手里抢过来。

00:44:11.300 --> 00:44:26.180
<v Claudia Perez>No, they were all just over there in the back of the gangway. They were talking, and he tried to rob my brother. And my brother tried to take the gun from him, and the guy did it. While my brother was trying to run, he shot him in the stomach, which hit his spinal cord.
<v Claudia Perez>等等，这个人是从哪儿出来的？他在车里吗？他在走路吗？

00:44:26.180 --> 00:45:09.410
<v Javier>Like I said, I was thinking. I was thinking that I had to get to the front of the house, because then if I lay here and bleed, I'll probably just bleed to death. I'll just bleed to death. So I figured that I would try to figure out a way to get to the front of the house. So I dragged myself on my arms. I dragged myself, and then some guy was passing by. I told him, "Hey, help me, I got shot." He didn't believe me. A big old commotion started happening. I caught an ambulance. And then all of a sudden, my wife and my kid and my sister just got there. I don't know how they found out I was there.
<v Javier>不，他们都在小巷后方。他们在谈话，他试图抢劫我哥哥。我哥哥试图从他手里抢枪，那个人就开枪了。我哥哥在试图逃跑时，他打中了我哥哥的肚子，击中了脊髓。

00:45:09.410 --> 00:45:47.620
<v Claudia Perez>When he was in the ICU unit, he looked real bad. And he was like all bloody, and blood was coming out, whatever. And he's dark-skinned, my brother. And at the ICU he looked pale. He had no color. And my mom was crying, because she's like, "Oh, my son. He's Negrito, like he's dark-skinned. Where's his color at? Where's his color?" And she was crying, "Where's my son's color at?" And begging, "Oh, my son's not dark no more. Got to give him his color back, because we will know that he's healthy with his color back."
<v Claudia Perez>就像我说的，我当时在思考。我在想我必须走到房子前面去，因为如果我躺在这儿流血，我可能会流血至死。我可能就会流血致死。所以我心想，我会想办法走到房子前面。所以我用双臂拖着自己。我拖着自己，然后有人经过。我告诉他，“嘿，帮帮我，我中枪了。”他不相信我。一场大混乱开始了。我叫了一辆救护车。然后突然，我的妻子、我的孩子和我妹妹都到了。我不知道他们是怎么发现我在那儿的。

00:45:47.620 --> 00:45:56.420
<v Javier>And it was three days, it was three or four days in the ICU. They came and told me that I'm going to be paralyzed.
<v Javier>他在重症监护室的时候，看起来很糟糕。他浑身都是血，血流了出来之类的。我的哥哥，他的肤色很深。在重症监护室他看起来很苍白。他一点血色都没有。我妈在哭，因为她说，“噢，我儿子。他是Negrito，肤色很深。他的血色呢？他的肤色去哪儿了？”她在哭，“我儿子的血色去哪儿了？”并在恳求，“噢，我儿子不再是深肤色了。必须把他的血色还给他，因为如果他的血色回来，我们就知道他健康了。”

00:45:56.420 --> 00:46:00.670
<v Claudia Perez>What was your reaction to that? How did it make you feel?
<v Claudia Perez>在重症监护室住了三天，是三四天吧。他们来告诉我我将瘫痪。

00:46:00.670 --> 00:46:11.550
<v Javier>I don't know. I didn't know how to react to it, because it had never happened to me. I mean, how am I supposed to know my [BLEEP] reaction?
<v Javier>你对那个的反应是什么？这让你感觉如何？

00:46:11.550 --> 00:46:41.220
<v Claudia Perez>He usually talks about it freely, nice. But I guess because it's me he won't talk about it normally, because me and him, we never talk about it. I want him to tell me, because I never asked him that before. And I wanted him to tell me something that I always wanted to know, and I was mad, because he gave me a dumb answer. I thought, then tell me something more--
<v Claudia Perez>我不知道。我不知道该怎么反应，因为它以前从未发生在我身上。我是说，我该怎么知道我他妈的反应？

00:46:41.220 --> 00:46:41.730
<v Ira Glass>Heartfelt.
<v Ira Glass>他通常谈论这个谈得很自由，很好。但我猜因为采访我的人是他，他不会正常谈论它，因为我和他，我们从来没谈过这个。我想要他说给我听，因为我从来没问过他。我想要他说一些我一直想知道的事，我很生气，因为他给了我一个愚蠢的答案。我想，那就告诉我一些更——

00:46:41.730 --> 00:46:50.750
<v Claudia Perez>Yeah, and he didn't tell me that. He just made me feel like, oh, my god, you're never going to change, or something.
<v Claudia Perez>发自肺腑的。

00:46:50.750 --> 00:46:53.000
<v Claudia Perez>What have you learned from this experience?
<v Claudia Perez>是的，他没有那样告诉我。他只是让我觉得，噢，天哪，你永远不会改变，之类的。

00:46:53.000 --> 00:47:09.650
<v Javier>Well, I learned a lot of new words. I don't know. I know a lot of things about spinal cords and stuff I never paid attention to. You know, like stuff like you really never cared about.
<v Javier>你从这段经历中学到了什么？

00:47:09.650 --> 00:47:14.940
<v Claudia Perez>Do you appreciate life more now?
<v Claudia Perez>好吧，我学到了很多新词。我不知道。我知道很多关于脊髓之类的我以前从没注意过的东西。你知道，就像你从来没在乎过的事情。

00:47:14.940 --> 00:47:17.780
<v Javier>It's the same.
<v Javier>你现在更珍惜生命了吗？

00:47:17.780 --> 00:47:20.220
<v Ira Glass>Do you think your brother's changed since the accident?
<v Ira Glass>一样。

00:47:20.220 --> 00:47:30.490
<v Claudia Perez>He's changed, yeah. He doesn't drink anymore. He thinks more about the future, like before he would go day by day.
<v Claudia Perez>你觉得你哥哥自从那次事故后变了吗？

00:47:30.490 --> 00:47:31.440
<v Ira Glass>He doesn't drink at all?
<v Ira Glass>他变了，是的。他不喝酒了。他更多地思考未来，比如以前他只是得过且过。

00:47:31.440 --> 00:47:37.350
<v Claudia Perez>Not at all, and you could drink when you're paralyzed. You could. He says he doesn't smoke.
<v Claudia Perez>他一点酒也不喝了吗？

00:47:37.350 --> 00:47:38.280
<v Ira Glass>And why? Why not?
<v Ira Glass>一点也不，你瘫痪了也是可以喝酒的。可以的。他说他不抽烟。

00:47:38.280 --> 00:48:12.440
<v Claudia Perez>He don't want to. He says that he feels stupid. He looks at-- before, he used to go out with his boys and all his friends. He's never seen how stupid they look. And now that he doesn't drink, he sees how stupid they are, and that there's no point to drink and get like that anymore. He says that-- one day he told me that if he ever walked again, he wouldn't take it for granted. He told me that once. He goes, I'll kiss the ground, or something like that. He told me once.
<v Claudia Perez>为什么？为什么不喝？

00:48:12.440 --> 00:48:50.700
<v Claudia Perez>And I was like, whoa, because we never talk like nice to each other. We're always fighting or something about something. And that one day that he told me that, I was like, oh. I felt weird. When he talks nice to me, it feels weird. Like when I was doing the interview, I couldn't look at him, because I was like, oh, you get on my nerves. It's that kind of thing, like we love each other, but we can't stand each other. And when we fight, he still beats me up, even though he's in a wheelchair. He still beats me up. I don't know. It's strange.
<v Claudia Perez>他不想。他说他觉得自己很蠢。他看看——以前，他总是和他的哥们和朋友们出去玩。他从未见过他们看起来有多蠢。现在他不喝酒了，他能看到他们有多蠢，而且再也没有意义去喝酒变得那样了。他说——有一天他告诉我，如果他能再次行走，他不会把它视为理所当然。他告诉过我一次。他说，我会亲吻大地，或者类似的话。他告诉我一次。

00:48:50.700 --> 00:48:51.640
<v Ira Glass>That hasn't changed.
<v Ira Glass>然后我当时就想，哇，因为我们从来不对彼此好说话。我们总是为了什么事情打架或者吵架。那天他告诉我那个，我当时就想，噢。我感觉很奇怪。当他对我好说话时，感觉很奇怪。就像我做采访的时候，我不敢看他，因为我在想，噢，你让我心烦。就是那种感觉，就像我们爱彼此，但我们受不了对方。当我们打架时，他还会打我，尽管他在轮椅里。他还是会打我。我不知道。这很奇怪。

00:48:51.640 --> 00:48:54.970
<v Claudia Perez>People tell me, how can he beat you up still? And I'm like, he does.
<v Claudia Perez>那点还没变。

00:48:54.970 --> 00:48:59.190
<v Ira Glass>How does he beat you up? Well, answer that. How can he pull that off?
<v Ira Glass>人们问我，他怎么还能打你？我说，他就是会打。

00:48:59.190 --> 00:49:48.440
<v Claudia Perez>I don't know. He does. I don't know. He's just real-- it's weird. It's like I learned after my brother's death experience, almost near, I wrote a poem to him. And I have it at home, but it's like I named it sick brother. And I said-- I don't remember exactly what I said in it, but I was writing letters to him when he was in the ICU unit, because I didn't want to go in there. I didn't want to go in there. And I told him when he was like laying there, and I told him I loved him. And I never told him that, but I had to tell him that. And I was like, man, in case something happens, I want him to know that even though he gets on my nerves, I do love my brother.
<v Claudia Perez>他怎么打你的？好吧，回答一下。他是怎么做到的？

00:49:48.440 --> 00:49:51.180
<v Ira Glass>And when you said this to him in the hospital, he was awake, and he could--
<v Ira Glass>我不知道。他就是会。我不知道。他就是非常——这很奇怪。就像我在我哥哥的死亡体验之后，几乎接近死亡，我写了一首诗给他。我在家有留底，但我把它命名为《生病的哥哥》。我说——我不记得我确切在诗里说了什么，但我写信给他是在他在重症监护室的时候，因为我不想进去。我不想进去。我在他躺在那儿的时候告诉他，我爱他。我从来没告诉过他，但我必须那样做。我在想，伙计，万一发生什么事，我要让他知道，尽管他让我心烦，我确实爱我的哥哥。

00:49:51.180 --> 00:49:52.080
<v Claudia Perez>No, I wrote it to him.
<v Claudia Perez>当你你在医院里对他说了这些，他醒着，他能——

00:49:52.080 --> 00:49:53.300
<v Ira Glass>You wrote it to him, and he saw it and he read it?
<v Ira Glass>不，我写信给他。

00:49:53.300 --> 00:49:54.350
<v Claudia Perez>He read it.
<v Claudia Perez>你写信给他，他看到了，读了？

00:49:54.350 --> 00:49:55.670
<v Ira Glass>And did he say anything about it afterwards?
<v Ira Glass>他读了。

00:49:55.670 --> 00:49:56.490
<v Claudia Perez>No.
<v Claudia Perez>他之后有说什么吗？

00:49:56.490 --> 00:49:58.530
<v Ira Glass>And then when he came out of the hospital, it was just like back to normal?
<v Ira Glass>没有。

00:49:58.530 --> 00:49:58.830
<v Claudia Perez>Normal.
<v Claudia Perez>然后当他出院时，一切就恢复正常了？

00:49:58.830 --> 00:49:59.810
<v Ira Glass>Like it never happened?
<v Ira Glass>正常。

00:49:59.810 --> 00:50:07.280
<v Claudia Perez>Yeah, like whatever. One day I told him, "Oh, I love you, brother." "ooh, get away from me." He told me, "Get away from me."
<v Claudia Perez>就像什么都没发生过？

00:50:07.280 --> 00:50:13.330
<v Ira Glass>So even nearly dying hasn't changed anything between the two of you?
<v Ira Glass>是的，就像随便吧。有一天我告诉他，“噢，我爱你，哥哥。”“噢，离我远点。”他告诉我说，“离我远点。”

00:50:13.330 --> 00:50:17.300
<v Claudia Perez>That is true, very true.
<v Claudia Perez>所以即使濒临死亡也没有改变你们俩之间的任何事？

00:50:17.300 --> 00:50:29.120
<v Claudia Perez>Is there any advice you would give to any young people out there who are trying to use their lives the same way you did, and they get up in the next incident like this?
<v Claudia Perez>那是真的，非常真实。

00:50:29.120 --> 00:50:44.340
<v Javier>Just don't be in the wrong place at the wrong time.
<v Javier>你有什么建议给那些正在以同样方式挥霍生命，并且可能在下一次事故中遭遇这种事的年轻人吗？

00:50:44.340 --> 00:51:13.650
<v Ira Glass>Claudia Perez is a college freshman at Moraine Valley majoring in fashion design. She also works as a hostess at the Continental House of Pancakes on Chicago's south side.
<v Ira Glass>别在错误的时间出现在错误的地点。

00:51:13.650 --> 00:51:43.330
<v Ira Glass>Act Four, Hope. A friend of a friend of mine was misdiagnosed with a disease and nearly died, and it got so bad that in the end she couldn't get out of bed, had said goodbye to everybody she knew, had given away everything, spent all her money. And she said that when the doctor told her you'll live, it was actually harder than when he told her you're going to die. She thought, what now? Well, Cheryl Trykv had a similar experience, and has our next story.
<v Ira Glass>克劳迪娅·佩雷斯是莫兰谷社区学院的大一学生，主修时装设计。她也在芝加哥南区的洲际煎饼屋担任领位员。

00:51:43.330 --> 00:52:15.580
<v Cheryl Trykv>Dr. Fargate tells me how lucky I am. He says surgery was touch and go, and that for an hour or so there I was this close-- with his fore and middle fingers side by side sticking out from the rest of his fist, he demonstrates the proximity. "You nearly died," he says, smiling, teasing me, "But it looks like you'll live." Oh? Why, that's wonderful. Life, one missed opportunity after the other, was this close.
<v Cheryl Trykv>第四幕，《希望》。我的一个朋友的朋友被误诊得了某种病，差点死掉，情况变得如此糟糕，以至于最后她下不了床，告别了她认识的每一个人，捐赠了一切，花光了所有的钱。她说当医生告诉她你会活下去时，这实际上比他告诉她你快要死掉时还要困难。她心想，现在该干什么？好吧，谢丽尔·特里夫也有类似的经历，并带来了我们的下一个故事。

00:52:15.580 --> 00:52:44.510
<v Cheryl Trykv>My roommate in the hospital tells me how lucky I am. She says that when she arrived there was no toilet paper or paper towels in the bathroom. "How am I supposed to wipe myself without no toilet paper?" she asked. Indeed. Great to be alive. Later I request a private room. But her line of thinking gets me asking questions of my own, big questions. The biggest questions come at meal time, such as, what is it?
<v Cheryl Trykv>法尔盖特医生告诉我我有多幸运。他说手术过程惊心动魄，那一两个小时里我离死只有这么一点——用他的食指和中指并排从拳头里伸出来，他演示了那个距离。“你差点死掉，”他笑着，打趣我说，“但看来你能活下去。”噢？哇，那太美妙了。生活，一个接一个错失的机会，竟然离我这么近。

00:52:44.510 --> 00:53:09.570
<v Cheryl Trykv>One evening the nurse comes in with my dinner tray. I situate myself upright in bed so as to feed myself, and lift the cover off the entree. They've served me my own pancreas. What's more is they try to pass it off as Italian beef. I tell the nurse there's been a mistake. I think I may have somebody else's tray. "No," she says, "no mistake."
<v Cheryl Trykv>医院里的室友告诉我我有多幸运。她说她来的时候卫生间里没有卫生纸或纸巾。“没卫生纸我怎么擦屁股？”她问。确实。活着真是太棒了。后来我要求换个单人病房。但她的思维逻辑让我自己也提出了问题，大问题。最大的问题出现在用餐时间，比如，这是什么？

00:53:09.570 --> 00:53:23.320
<v Cheryl Trykv>So many questions. So many, many questions. Oh, the morphine drip provides five or six brilliant answers, but I can't remember them now.
<v Cheryl Trykv>一天晚上护士送来了我的餐盘。我把自己撑在床上以便喂自己吃，掀开了主菜的盖子。他们端给我我自己的胰腺。更重要的是，他们试图把它当作意大利炖牛肉蒙混过关。我告诉护士出了点错。我想我可能拿错了别人的餐盘。“不，”她说，“没搞错。”

00:53:23.320 --> 00:53:46.070
<v Cheryl Trykv>I blather away the hours in a semi-fog of hope that maybe the doctors missed something. Maybe I'm not so lucky after all. Maybe they're wrong about me. Maybe I won't have to return to that world of tending bar, and waiting tables, and customers with their selfish tastes and relentless hungers for penny ante attention. No more train and bus connections.
<v Cheryl Trykv>太多的问题。太多太多的问题。噢，吗啡点滴提供了五六个绝妙的答案，但我现在记不起来了。

00:53:46.070 --> 00:54:20.910
<v Cheryl Trykv>The flowers and get well cards will keep coming in all day long, the few days I'll have remaining. And friends will flock in from the trees or wherever it is they've been hiding, and tell me over and over again how much they love me. They'll tell me not so much in words but in the way they massage my feet, get me ice cream, and bring me gifts. "Not another book on the mind-body connection. Oh, put it with the stack of others there in the corner. Did you find out if Toshiro had any more of those little bed jackets for me?"
<v Cheryl Trykv>我在一种希望医生可能搞错了什么的半梦半醒的状态中虚度光阴。也许我根本没那么幸运。也许他们关于我的诊断错了。也许我不必回到那个服务员、餐桌侍应生的世界，回到那些有自私品味、无休止渴望着一点点关注的顾客身边。再也没有火车和公交线路了。

00:54:20.910 --> 00:54:29.720
<v Cheryl Trykv>Dr. Fargate comes and goes with his probing brigade. I think the Pakistani has a crush on me. Anyway, he's barely an intern.
<v Cheryl Trykv>鲜花和康复贺卡会整天不停地送来，在我生命仅剩的那几天里。朋友们会从树林或任何他们躲藏的地方涌来，一遍又一遍地告诉我他们有多爱我。他们告诉我的不是用语言，而是用他们按摩我脚的方式、给我买冰淇淋、给我带礼物。“不是又一本关于身心联结的书吗？噢，把它堆在角落里那一摞书里吧。你发现托希罗还有没有更多那种小外套给我？”

00:54:29.720 --> 00:54:53.320
<v Cheryl Trykv>Later Dr. Fargate returns alone with something serious to tell me. "We've taken a second look at your biopsy report. I'm afraid we're looking at stage four. It's critical." "Oh, thank God. I thought you were going to talk about my bill." He tells me at this point I should do anything to lift my spirits.
<v Cheryl Trykv>法尔盖特医生带着他的问诊团队来来去去。我觉得那个巴基斯坦人对我有好感。不管怎样，他只是个实习生。

00:54:53.320 --> 00:55:24.430
<v Cheryl Trykv>That night there is a champagne party in my room for the entire ward. I let one of the nurses wear my bed cap, the one with mink along the brim. A few of us are busted for smoking in the bathroom. Drunk on Fools Wit long after the others have returned to their rooms, I prank phone call various former employers, leaving messages of hell-born prophecy on their voice mail. I make a list of hardships and collection agencies I will escape if I were to die. How soon is now?
<v Cheryl Trykv>后来法尔盖特医生独自回来，有件严肃的事要告诉我。“我们重新看了你的活检报告。我恐怕我们看到的是四期。情况危急。”“噢，感谢上帝。我还以为你要谈我的账单了。”他告诉我在此刻我应该做任何事来振奋精神。

00:55:24.430 --> 00:56:06.860
<v Cheryl Trykv>At night I dream I am a beekeeper developing a strain of worker bees that automatically die before their pension fund kicks in. Because the fund only amounts to a thimble full of pollen, the early death is useful. I wake with the image of me standing before a subcommittee selling a special gas made from identical molecular bee genetics that can be sprayed on postal employees. I hold a sign that reads, works until drops dead.
<v Cheryl Trykv>那天晚上，我在病房里为整个病区举办了一场香槟派对。我让其中一个护士戴上我的睡帽，帽檐上有貂皮的那顶。我们有几个人因为在卫生间抽烟被抓住了。在其他人回到病房很久之后，我仍然沉醉在愚人智慧中，恶作剧电话打给几位前雇主，在他们的语音信箱里留下地狱般的预言信息。我列了一份清单，写着如果我死了我将逃避的苦难和讨债公司。现在有多快？

00:56:06.860 --> 00:56:10.010
<v Ira Glass>Act Five, On the Edge of the World.
<v Ira Glass>晚上我梦见自己是一个养蜂人，正在培育一种会自动死掉的工蜂，在养老金生效之前就死掉。因为基金只有一顶针那么多的花粉，过早死亡很有用。醒来时，我的脑海中浮现出我站在小组委员会前，兜售一种由相同的分子蜂遗传基因制成的特殊气体，可以喷在邮政员工身上。我举着一个写着“工作直到倒毙”的牌子。

00:56:10.010 --> 00:56:15.450
<v Matthew Goulish>How do I sustain interest in a world that I'm scheduled not to be a part of very soon?
<v Matthew Goulish>第五幕，《世界边缘》。

00:56:15.450 --> 00:56:39.080
<v Ira Glass>Matthew Goulish says that when he  had cancer, he found that he couldn't cry. He would hear bad news about people who he knew, fellow patients who he heard had died, and he says that he just didn't feel much. It was as if he had arrived at a state where all the events of the world seemed of equal weight, of equal importance. Well, we thought we would give Matthew the last word in today's program.
<v Ira Glass>我怎么能在某种很快就被安排不再参与的世界中保持兴趣？

00:56:39.080 --> 00:56:52.660
<v Matthew Goulish>In 1994, six years after I had been cured, an acquaintance of mine contracted lymphoma. I wrote her a letter which I never delivered-- until now.
<v Matthew Goulish>马修·古利什说，当他得了癌症时，他发现自己哭不出来。他听到关于他认识的人，那些一同住院的病人去世的消息，他说他感觉不到太多。这就好比他抵达了一种状态，世界上的所有事件似乎都具有相同的分量、相同的重要性。好吧，我们想把今天节目的最后发言权留给马修。

00:56:52.660 --> 00:57:35.400
<v Matthew Goulish>Dear Brigid, as our small party waited for the table, talking among ourselves at the Greek restaurant last night, I noticed you looking at the display of fresh foods-- heaps of hazelnuts, walnuts, leaves of oregano, bottles of oils on the dark wood cutting block. You watched the cook chopping up onions, and I recognized the look on your face. Events had equalized for you. You had been pulled three quarters out of the world, and I wanted to say what's happening to you is not fair. It's not time yet, and statements of that kind.
<v Matthew Goulish>1994年，在我治愈六年后，我的一位熟人患上了淋巴瘤。我写了一封信给她，我从未寄出——直到现在。

00:57:35.400 --> 00:58:05.490
<v Matthew Goulish>But when you are leaving the world you understand that it isn't death that is strange and fearsome, but life. How is this struggling life possible? How are these people possible? How am I possible? I am not.
<v Matthew Goulish>亲爱的布里吉德，昨晚在我们的小团体在希腊餐厅等待餐桌时，大家在谈话，我注意到你在看着陈列的新鲜食物——成堆的榛子、核桃、牛至叶、暗色木头砧板上的油瓶。你看着厨师切洋葱，我认出了你脸上的那种神情。事件对你来说已经拉平了。你已经被拉出了世界四分之三的距离，我想说你所经历的事情是不公平的。现在还不到时候，以及这类陈述。

00:58:05.490 --> 00:58:16.760
<v Ira Glass>Our program was produced by Nancy Updike, Alix Spiegel, and myself. Contributing editors Paul Tough, Jack Hitt, and Margy Rochlin. Music help from John Connors, Peter Margasak, and Steve Cushing. Thanks to Bridget Murphy.
<v Ira Glass>但当你离开这个世界时，你明白奇怪和可怕的不是死亡，而是生活。这种挣扎的生活怎么可能存在？这些人怎么可能存在？我怎么可能存在？我并不存在。

00:58:16.760 --> 00:58:30.350
<v Ira Glass>The first version of this episode of our radio show aired back in 1995. In the years since then, I am sorry to report Lawrence Steger, the writer and performer in Act Two, has died. A very wonderful man.
<v Ira Glass>我们的节目由南希·厄普代克、亚历克斯·斯皮格尔和我本人制作。特约编辑保罗·塔夫、杰克·希特和玛吉·罗克林。音乐帮助来自约翰·康纳斯、彼得·马加萨克和史蒂夫·库欣。感谢布里奇特·墨菲。

00:58:30.350 --> 00:58:33.340
<v Ira Glass>This American Life is distributed by Public Radio International.
<v Ira Glass>这档广播节目的第一集版本早在1995年就播出了。在随后的岁月里，我很遗憾地报告，第二幕中的作家和表演者劳伦斯·斯蒂格已经去世。一个非常出色的人。

00:58:33.340 --> 00:58:46.140
<v Ira Glass>[FUNDING CREDITS]
<v Ira Glass>《美国生活》由国际公共广播电台发行。

00:58:46.140 --> 00:58:54.800
<v Ira Glass>WBEZ management oversight by Torey Malatia. I'm Ira Glass. Back next week with more stories of This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>[资助名单]

00:58:54.800 --> 00:58:59.800
<v Announcer>PRI, Public Radio International.
<v Announcer>WBEZ管理监督由托里·马拉蒂亚负责。我是艾拉·格拉斯。下周带着更多《美国生活》的故事回来。
